            <ArticleTitle>Type II enteropathy-associated T-cell lymphoma: a multicenter analysis from the Asia Lymphoma Study Group.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>663-8</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1002/ajh.23213</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Enteropathy-associated T-cell lymphoma (EATL) is a rare primary gastrointestinal T-cell lymphoma. A multicenter study from the Asia Lymphoma Study Group identified 38 EATL patients within a 19-year period. All cases were type II EATL. Men were affected twice as common as women, at a median age of 59 (23-89) years. None had a history of celiac disease. The sites of involvement were small bowel and stomach (5%), small bowel (63%), small and large bowel (16%), and large bowel (18%). Common presenting features were bowel perforation (34%), pain (32%), and obstruction (21%). Lymphomas showed monomorphic neoplastic lymphoid infiltrates that were CD3⁺ (100%), CD56⁺ (91%), TIA-1⁺ (96%), CD4⁻CD8⁺ (63%), CD4⁺CD8⁺ (19%), CD4⁻CD8⁻ (16%), and CD4⁺CD8⁻ (3%). Epstein Barr virus was demonstrable in three cases. Despite chemotherapy and/or surgical resection, the overall response and complete response rates were poor at 46% and 38%. The median overall survival (OS) was 7 months and progression-free-survival (PFS) 1 month. Five patients underwent hematopoietic stem cell transplantation all were alive. Age and the prognostic index for peripheral T-cell lymphoma were not prognostically significant. Good performance status was associated with better OS (P = 0.03), and response to initial treatment led to better OS and PFS (P &lt; 0.001).</AbstractText>
